<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oprichnina</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1565">1565</a>–<a href="/years/1572">1572</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The oprichnina was a state policy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> Ivan the Terrible in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> from 1565 to 1572 that included mass repression of the boyars, the Russian aristocracy, with public executions and the confiscation of their land and property. During the costly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-war">Livonian War</a>, and after Prince Andrey Kurbsky defected in 1564, Ivan announced his abdication and returned only on condition that he could prosecute traitors outside legal limits. He ruled a separate territory alone through his guard, the oprichniki, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boyar">Boyar</a> Council ruled the zemshchina. The oprichniki carried out a month-long terror in Novgorod, and in 1572 the two halves of the state were reunited.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oprichnina.jpg" alt="Oprichnina" width="330" height="222" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nikolai Nevrev · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:0NevrevNV_Oprichniki_BISH.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan IV</a> began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-war">Livonian War</a> in 1558, and a broad coalition including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> was drawn in against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. The war lasted until 1583, and raids by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-tatars">Crimean Tatars</a>, Polish and Lithuanian invasions, famines and a trading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> ravaged the country. In 1564 Prince Andrey Kurbsky defected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a> and led their army against Russia, devastating the region of Velikiye Luki, and Ivan began to suspect other aristocrats of a readiness to betray him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 3 December 1564 Ivan left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> on a pilgrimage with a large guard, many boyars and the treasury. In letters of 3 January 1565 from Aleksandrova Sloboda he accused the elite of embezzlement and treason and announced his abdication. Envoys begged him to return, and he agreed on condition that he could prosecute traitors without interference from the boyar council or the church. He raised a levy of 100,000 rubles to pay for the oprichnina, originally a term for land left to a noble widow apart from her children's land.</p><p>The oprichnina territory lay mostly in the former Novgorod Republic. Ivan also formed the oprichniki, originally a thousand strong, who swore not to eat or drink with the zemshchina. In 1566 the oprichnina was extended to eight central districts, where 570 of 12,000 nobles became oprichniki and the rest were expelled. In Novgorod the oprichniki raided the town and executed people of all classes; an estimated 1500 nobles were killed, though the true number is unknown. The persecutions then turned on the oprichnina's own leaders, Aleksei Basmanov and Afanasy Viazemsky.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In 1571 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-tatars">Crimean Tatars</a> burnt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, and the oprichniki offered no serious resistance. In 1572 the two territories were reunited under a reformed Boyar Council with members from both sides. Scholars explain the end in different ways: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> success may have shaken Ivan's faith in the institution, or he may have judged that the weakening of the princely elite was achieved.</p><p>Historians also disagree about its purpose. Vasily Klyuchevsky and Stepan Veselovsky saw Ivan's paranoia, Sergey Platonov a suppression of the rising boyar aristocracy, and Isabel de Madariaga an attempt to subordinate every independent class to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/autocracy">autocracy</a>. The break-up of large estates put peasants under stricter landowners, and the number of itinerant people grew, which may have led to the institutionalisation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oprichnina” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
